AB  - This study aims to examine the drivers and barriers to Gen-AI (ChatGPT) adoption among tourism and hospitality researchers, using Behavioral Reasoning Theory (BRT), and to identify context-specific factors that influence their adoption. The study employed a mixed-method design. It identified subfactors labeled “reason for,” including time savings, knowledge creation, cost reduction, and low complexity, and “reasons against,” such as ethical concerns, privacy issues, and information currency, regarding tourism and hospitality researchers' attitudes and their adoption of Gen-AI (ChatGPT). Additionally, the findings confirm that openness to innovation has an insignificant effect on reasons against adoption but a significant effect on reasons for adoption and on attitudes toward adoption intentions for Gen-AI (ChatGPT). Attitudes significantly mediate the relationship between reasons and intention. This research enhances understanding of researchers' attitudes and adoption intentions toward Gen-AI (ChatGPT) in academic research by applying Behavioral Reasoning Theory (BRT) and a mixed-methods approach that integrates reasons for and against adoption within a single framework.
